46: <div id="puzzle" class="hidden"><div class="panel"><div class="eyebrow">The sanctum · The final offering</div><h2>Set the story in order</h2><p>The old inscription asks for the three signs of that storm. Read your notes. What came first, what was protected, and what returned?</p><div class="symbols"><button class="sym" data-sym="rain">☔ &nbsp; Rain</button><button class="sym" data-sym="seed">❋ &nbsp; Seed</button><button class="sym" data-sym="sun">☼ &nbsp; Sun</button></div><div class="sequence" id="sequence">Choose three signs</div><div class="puzzle-actions"><button class="btn" id="offerBtn">Offer the signs</button><button class="btn ghost" id="resetPuzzle">Reset</button></div><div class="message" id="puzzleMessage"></div></div></div>
47: <div id="ending" class="hidden"><div class="panel"><div class="eyebrow">Truth recorded · Case closed</div><h2>The last lamp is free.</h2><p class="storyline">Valli was the temple's lamp keeper and the village healer. She discovered that the headman had diverted the spring and forged the land records, leaving the lower hamlet to drown when the monsoon dam failed. She hid the copper deed and warning in the sanctum, then rang the bell to lead children to high ground. The doors were barred from outside. The village called her a thief to hide its shame.</p><p>Her blood was not a curse. It was the mark of a truth no one would hear. You have given her name back—and witnessed what was done.</p><div class="rule"></div><p style="color:#d9bd80">At dawn, the lamp gutters out. The temple doors stand open. On the threshold, one anklet glints in the sun.</p><button class="btn" id="returnBtn">Return to the village</button></div></div>
